      "content": "The word “mute” is a useful clue to notice, but it does not by itself make the figure personification. Look at how the ideas in the lines are linked: the lines use a **metaphor**, rather than simply giving a non-human thing a human action.\n\n**Model answer:** The figure of speech is a **metaphor**. (1)\n\n### 📖 Concept & Thought Process (How to Understand It):\n* **The Concept / Rule:** A metaphor directly presents one thing as another, transferring an idea or quality between them. Personification specifically gives human qualities or actions to something non-human.\n* **The Thought Process (How to Unpack It):** Read the whole expression, not just one word. Ask whether the lines make a direct figurative comparison or mainly give a non-human thing a human quality. Here, the expected figure is a metaphor.\n* **How DBE Markers Score It:** This question asks you to identify the figure of speech, so the mark is for naming **metaphor** correctly.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** A single word that sounds human does not automatically mean personification. Use the relationship between the ideas in the full line to identify the figure."
