      "reasoning": "The learner gives a valid reason to hold Absalom responsible, but treats that reason as ruling out pity altogether. Respond to this claim, then model an answer that weighs his difficult circumstances against his choices and reaches a clear judgement.",
      "content": "His good upbringing is a relevant reason to hold him responsible, but it does not automatically mean that he cannot be pitied. A strong response weighs his circumstances against his choices.\n\n**Model answer:** Absalom Kumalo can be pitied to some extent. He faces unemployment and fear during the crime, and he is the one who ultimately pays the price. These circumstances make his situation tragic. However, he was raised with good values and had an opportunity to change at the reformatory. He also chose to carry a gun and take part in the crime. I therefore feel some pity for Absalom, but I do not believe his circumstances remove his responsibility for his choices.\n\n### 📖 Concept & Thought Process (How to Understand It):\n* **The Concept / Rule:** “Discuss your view” requires a clear opinion supported by relevant details from the novel. You may acknowledge more than one side.\n* **The Thought Process (How to Unpack It):** State your view; consider reasons to pity Absalom, such as unemployment and fear; consider reasons to question that pity, such as his choices; then explain your overall judgement.\n* **How DBE Markers Score It:** Markers look for a clear, relevant response, accurate supporting details, and an explanation that connects those details to your opinion. There is more than one acceptable viewpoint.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Avoid simply retelling events. Explain how each detail supports your view."
